Maybe `step.url_template` uses a hostname like `{{base_url}}`? At compile time unresolved, `new URL` throws, fakeRailsTarget false; then `compileUrl` resolves to HTTP URL but requireHttps remains true because fakeRailsTarget was computed before parameter substitution. This is a bug: `step.url_template` may be `{{api}}/...` with base_url parameter. Need compute fakeRailsTarget after compileUrl. Same in ApiConsumer? There `request.url` likely already resolved. StepExecutor computes on raw template before substitution. Fix: compile URL first with `requireHttps: false`? Better compile with default then validate after; compute fakeRailsTarget on compiled URL and re-validate. Implement:
```
const compiled = compileUrl(..., { allowedHosts, requireHttps: false, ...});
const parsedCandidate = new URL(compiled);
const fakeRailsTarget = isFakeRailsHostAllowed(parsedCandidate.hostname, allowedHosts);
const url = compileUrl(step.url_template, query, { allowedHosts, requireHttps: !fakeRailsTarget, ...});
```
But first compile with requireHttps false permits HTTP even if not fake; then second compile with requireHttps based on fake. Good. For non-fake HTTP, second compile throws. Patch StepExecutor. Also `assertUrlAllowed` already uses final URL; good.

Patch around 792.

